    "q-49685": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*",
      "worked_solution": "First, factoring the denominator and using partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}=\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)}=\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {s}_{2} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ {s}_{3} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ {s}_{4} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
