    "q-119161": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
