    "q-84409": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Kevin is moving his car in a straight line. His position relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function $x(t)=2{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{m},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find its average position between $2$ and $6$ seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the average value of the position on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}\\,{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{6-2}\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}2{e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2\\cdot 2({e}^{t/2})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6/2}-{e}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average position is $({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
