    "q-122387": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/b8e332d7f53868313cc589e4ce5e991d2bcce88d15613111c4d0f920b678830d.png)\n\nWhich expression gives the total finite area enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{4}$ and $x={y}^{2},$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(y)={y}^{4}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{4}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/8b4cbf07b9c2a6d949a58f39489889d2a3fdd4690664e530386f6f61d9aac5fd.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-1,0],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [0,1],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-122387-a",
              "type": "math",
