    "q-118775": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
