    "q-50330": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/31d4236bf58cd579c49c7259c32a2a9171e07c771b4c34040ac92896809f695f.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ and $x=3-y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y} & =3-y \\\\ 2 & =3y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/b73c341f4d09b1df7bea162bc7dbe10d0ee60920c847f9c24210386a3d139e61.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/c8c8e766c66e47a54f316f54893d603b245acec8f529da3a5033d2bba547a319.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3-y)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/1b31331f1c0da353bee6fe29d10e9e58dd531cbc8152cbce214d1adea771b032.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{2}{y})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
