    "q-83470": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 21",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,1)$ and has velocity $⟨2,4⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3{e}^{3t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3t}+t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3(0)}+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{3},\\,1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}={C}_{4}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
