    "q-83878": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 23",
      "problem": "The rate of growth in the frog population $P(t)$ in a certain lake follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P(5\\,000-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Find an expression for the frog population after $t$ years, given that there were $100$ frogs initially.",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.1)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5\\,000}{100} \\\\ C & =50-1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
